<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Lauron</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">76 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sertorian-war">Sertorian War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Lauron was fought in 76 BCE in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sertorian-war">Sertorian War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Quintus Sertorius</a> besieged the town of Lauron and outmanoeuvred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s relief army, destroying part of it, and then took and burned the town. The defeat was Pompey's first major setback in Spain.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Sertorius, a client of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a>, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for his province in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> in 82 BCE. Driven out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>'s general Gaius Annius Luscus, he went to Mauretania, where he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tingis</a>, until the Lusitanians asked him to lead them; he returned in 80 BCE and defeated several Sullan armies. The Senate sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-pius">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius</a> against him, and in 76 BCE Pompey with a still larger army. That year Marcus Perpenna joined Sertorius with the remnants of the army of Marcus Aemilius Lepidus, and Sertorius turned against the east coast, whose cities supported his enemies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Lauron lay between the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> and Metellus. Sertorius arrived first and besieged it. Pompey, with 30,000 infantry and 1,000 cavalry, camped close by and invited the townspeople to watch from their walls, believing Sertorius caught between the town and his army. Sertorius had his light troops and cavalry harass Pompey's nearby foragers while sparing those further off, until the foraging moved to the distant tracts. He then laid an ambush with ten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a> of heavy infantry, ten of light troops and two thousand cavalry under Octavius Gracinus and Tarquitius Priscus.</p><p>The returning foragers were routed and cut down. A legion sent under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s legate Decimus Laelius to cover them was struck in the rear by cavalry and in front by infantry, and broke in turn. When Pompey led out his army, Sertorius drew up his remaining troops, and Pompey, unable to move without exposing his flank or rear, stayed where he was. Pompey's army lost ten thousand men, and Sertorius burned Lauron.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The arrival of Metellus forced Sertorius to withdraw, so the victory was not decisive, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s army remained a force to be reckoned with. The war went on for several more years and ended only when some of Sertorius's own men plotted against him and assassinated him. The battle is described in detail by Frontinus in his Stratagems and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> in his Lives of Sertorius and Pompey.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Lauron” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He allied with Mithridates VI of Pontus and beat Pompey at <b>Lauron</b> in 76 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/sertorian-state">Sertorian State</a></li></ul></section>
